OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F496A1774395394
OL,1,Vk4                               G4/24
OL,2,Vj5{5jj5~sG"BETTER WAYS OF RAISING
OL,3,Vj5op0ozz5pGMONEY" SAYS HESELTINE
OL,4,D
OL,5, The Environment Secretary has admitted
OL,6, the Government could have administered
OL,7, the poll tax better.
OL,9,FMichael Heseltine said things would
OL,10,Fhave been better if it had foreseen the
OL,11,Fproblem of collectability before the
OL,12,Fcommunity charge was introduced.
OL,13,F
OL,14,FSpeaking in a BBC interview for On The
OL,15,FRecord, he admitted he had believed
OL,16,Fthere were better ways of raising
OL,17,Fthe sums of money.
OL,18,F
OL,19,FHe said some elements of the tax had
OL,20,Fbeen a mistake but it was important not
OL,21,Fto lose sight of what it tried to do.
OL,22,F
OL,23,D]FFromG24-03-91F/ teletextarchive.com
OL,24,ANext PageBT'text FilesCRewindFReg Radio
FL,497,190,120,250,0,100
PN,49605
SC,0005
CT,25,T
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F496A1774395394
OL,1,Vk4                               G5/24
OL,2,Vj5{5jj5~sGHESELTINE DENIES POWER
OL,3,Vj5op0ozz5pGBEING CENTRALISED
OL,4,D
OL,5, The Environment Secretary has denied
OL,6, the shift in local government funding
OL,7, to cut poll tax bills means that power
OL,8, is being centralised.
OL,10,FMichael Heseltine, in an interview for
OL,11,FBBC's On the Record, said the reforms
OL,12,Fin fact meant that power was being
OL,13,Ftransferred to the people.
OL,15,FHe said this had begun by enabling
OL,16,Fcouncil tenants to own their own houses
OL,17,Fand giving parents and teachers a
OL,18,Fbigger role in locally-managed schools.
OL,20,FFar from being centralisation, it was
OL,21,F"an explosion of localism", he said.
OL,23,D]FFromG24-03-91F/ teletextarchive.com
OL,24,ANext PageBT'text FilesCRewindFReg Radio
FL,497,190,120,250,0,100
PN,49606
SC,0006
CT,25,T
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F496A1774395394
OL,1,Vk4                               G6/24
OL,2,Vj5{5jj5~sGLAMONT DENIES THATCHERISM
OL,3,Vj5op0ozz5pGDEING DUMPED
OL,5, Chancellor Norman Lamont has denied
OL,6, claims that the abolition of the poll
OL,7, tax and the terms of the latest budget
OL,8, represent a U-turn on Thatcherism.
OL,9,F
OL,10,FSpeaking on LWT's Walden programme, he
OL,11,Fsaid: "Our policies are evolving from
OL,12,Fthose of Mrs Thatcher".
OL,13,F
OL,14,F"We still believe in reducing taxation,
OL,15,Fand have kept the principle that
OL,16,Findividuals should contribute to the
OL,17,Fnew local tax," he declared.
OL,18,F
OL,19,FOn the issue of child benefit, which is
OL,20,Fnow to be increased, Mr Lamont said it
OL,21,Fhas been set for review for some time.
OL,22,D
OL,23,D]FFromG24-03-91F/ teletextarchive.com
OL,24,ANext PageBT'text FilesCRewindFReg Radio
FL,497,190,120,250,0,100
PN,49607
SC,0007
CT,25,T
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F496A1774395394
OL,1,Vk4                               G7/24
OL,2,Vj5{5jj5~sGHESELTINE'S "POLL TAX
OL,3,Vj5op0ozz5pGBLUNDER" - LABOUR
OL,5, Labour has accused the Environment
OL,6, Secretary of being "incompetent and
OL,7, ignorant" over his handling of the poll
OL,8, tax abolition.
OL,9,F
OL,10,FShadow Environment Secretary Bryan
OL,11,FGould claimed Michael Heseltine could
OL,12,Fnot answer basic questions about his
OL,13,Fplans during a BBC-TV interview today.
OL,14,F
OL,15,FHe said Mr Heseltine took refuge in
OL,16,F"shifty evasions" when asked whether
OL,17,Fa poll tax register would be kept.
OL,19,FMr Gould accused the Environment
OL,20,FSecretary of being "ignorant" of the
OL,21,Fworkings of local government.
OL,22,F
OL,23,D]FFromG24-03-91F/ teletextarchive.com
